      "text": "We solve the system of equations by using either the substitution method or the elimination method. The equations are already in a standard form, so we can use the elimination method.\nWe want to cancel the variable $y$ out of the equations, so we need to find a common multiple of the coefficients of $y$. The least common multiple of 5 and 2 is 10. We can multiply equation (1) by 2 and equation (2) by 5 to eliminate $y$:\n\\begin{align*}\n(3x-5y)&= -11,\\\\\n7x+2y&=-12.\n\\end{align*}\nMultiply equation (1) by 2:\n\\begin{align*}\n6x - 10y &= -22.\n\\end{align*}\nMultiply equation (2) by 5:\n\\begin{align*}\n35x + 10y &= -60.\n\\end{align*}\nNow we can add the two equations to eliminate $y$:\n\\begin{align*}\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) &= -22 + (-60) \\\\\n41x &= -82.\n\\end{align*}\nDivide both sides by 41 to solve for $x$:\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= -\\frac{82}{41}.\n\\end{align*}\nNow that we have the value of $x$, we can substitute it into one of the original equations to solve for $y$. We'll start with equation (1):\n\\begin{align*}\n3(-\\frac{82}{41}) - 5y &= -11 \\\\\n-\\frac{246}{41} - 5y &= -11.\n\\end{align*}\nTo get rid of the fraction, multiply both sides of the equation by 41:\n\\begin{align*}\n-246 - 205y &= -451 \\\\\n-205y &= -205 \\\\\ny &= 1.\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $(x,y) = \\boxed{(-\\frac{82}{41}, 1)}$.\nThe answer is: (-\\frac{82}{41},1)",
